{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V24wqrUvNrBveoX7GTTU5g","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl QC-fail). Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"sft_steps\": 3650,\n7\t      \"sft_final_loss\": 1.555,\n8\t      \"sft_token_accuracy\": 0.672,\n9\t      \"training_time_min\": 60,\n10\t      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.261,\n11\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.362,\n12\t      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.305,\n13\t      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.061,\n14\t      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.028,\n15\t      \"gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.003,\n16\t      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.011,\n17\t      \"s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000\": 0.69,\n18\t      \"s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000\": 0.5,\n19\t      \"s1_auc_improvement\": 0.19,\n20\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit\": 0.08,\n21\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci\": [\n22\t        -0.85,\n23\t        1.0\n24\t      ],\n25\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n26\t      \"gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly\": 0.032,\n27\t      \"gsm8k_cal_mae_all\": 0.037\n28\t    },\n29\t    \"tables\": [\n30\t      {\n31\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)\",\n32\t        \"columns\": [\n33\t          \"Metric\",\n34\t          \"d100-All\",\n35\t          \"d100-QC-Only\",\n36\t          \"Delta\",\n37\t          \"Interpretation\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"rows\": [\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"MMLU Accuracy\",\n42\t            0.233,\n43\t            0.261,\n44\t            0.028,\n45\t            \"Less capacity displacement\"\n46\t          ],\n47\t          [\n48\t            \"ARC Accuracy\",\n49\t            0.294,\n50\t            0.305,\n51\t            0.011,\n52\t            \"Reduced noisy SFT interference\"\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            \"GSM8K Accuracy\",\n56\t            0.365,\n57\t            0.362,\n58\t            -0.003,\n59\t            \"Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\"\n60\t          ],\n61\t          [\n62\t            \"S1 AUC (d100 vs d000)\",\n63\t            0.5,\n64\t            0.69,\n65\t            0.19,\n66\t            \"Label noise reduction\"\n67\t          ],\n68\t          [\n69\t            \"GSM8K Cal MAE\",\n70\t            0.037,\n71\t            0.032,\n72\t            -0.005,\n73\t            \"Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\"\n74\t          ]\n75\t        ]\n76\t      }\n77\t    ],\n78\t    \"figures\": [],\n79\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact, not signal degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However, 0.5B MC data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\\n\\n\\nQC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination purity.\\n(1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because ALL items are labeled \\\"contaminated\\\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually have high-quality paraphrases. The signal can't discriminate because labels are noisy. QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set, so S1 now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated on the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100 the model's representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items), but much better than d100-all's labeling artifact.\\n(2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547 QC-pass items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU contamination, but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity displacement are removed. ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass ARC items provide concentrated forgetting signal but less total capacity displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319 GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination in either condition.\\n(3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly matters for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\\nPaper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability. Reported as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The S1 AUC improvement (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling artifact) by showing the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\\n\"\n80\t  },\n81\t  {\n82\t    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n83\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100), 1 seed. Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run). Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\\n\",\n84\t    \"results\": {\n85\t      \"sft_time_per_run_hours\": 6.5,\n86\t      \"total_gpu_hours\": 32.5,\n87\t      \"lora_adapter_size_mb\": 210,\n88\t      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.724,\n89\t      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.793,\n90\t      \"d000_arc\": 0.896,\n91\t      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.268,\n92\t      \"d005_mmlu\": 0.725,\n93\t      \"d005_gsm8k\": 0.791,\n94\t      \"d005_arc\": 0.895,\n95\t      \"d005_humaneval\": 0.274,\n96\t      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.726,\n97\t      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.795,\n98\t      \"d025_arc\": 0.892,\n99\t      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.28,\n100\t      \"d050_mmlu\": 0.727,\n101\t      \"d050_gsm8k\": 0.796,\n102\t      \"d050_arc\": 0.89,\n103\t      \"d050_humaneval\": 0.287,\n104\t      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.729,\n105\t      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.8,\n106\t      \"d100_arc\": 0.887,\n107\t      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.293,\n108\t      \"mmlu_delta_d100\": 0.005,\n109\t      \"gsm8k_delta_d100\": 0.007,\n110\t      \"arc_delta_d100\": -0.009,\n111\t      \"humaneval_delta_d100\": 0.025,\n112\t      \"s1_d005_auc_7b\": 0.97,\n113\t      \"s2_d005_auc_7b\": 0.51,\n114\t      \"s2_d100_cohens_d_7b\": -0.42,\n115\t      \"s4_d005_auc_7b\": 0.53,\n116\t      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl\": 0.52,\n117\t      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci\": [\n118\t        -0.12,\n119\t        1.18\n120\t      ],\n121\t      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n122\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl\": 0.05,\n123\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci\": [\n124\t        -0.28,\n125\t        0.38\n126\t      ],\n127\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n128\t      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl\": 0.58,\n129\t      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci\": [\n130\t        -0.22,\n131\t        1.4\n132\t      ],\n133\t      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n134\t      \"coverage_95_arc_n24\": 0.625,\n135\t      \"coverage_95_gsm8k_n32\": 0.688,\n136\t      \"coverage_95_mmlu_n24\": 0.667,\n137\t      \"kendall_tau_arc_n24\": 0.79,\n138\t      \"kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32\": 0.83,\n139\t      \"scale_modulation\": {\n140\t        \"arc_05b\": -17.5,\n141\t        \"arc_15b\": -0.7,\n142\t        \"arc_3b\": -0.3,\n143\t        \"arc_7b\": -0.9,\n144\t        \"mmlu_05b\": -16.5,\n145\t        \"mmlu_15b\": 0.8,\n146\t        \"mmlu_3b\": 0.8,\n147\t        \"mmlu_7b\": 0.5,\n148\t        \"gsm8k_05b\": 0.1,\n149\t        \"gsm8k_15b\": -0.5,\n150\t        \"gsm8k_3b\": 0.2,\n151\t        \"gsm8k_7b\": 0.7\n152\t      }\n153\t    },\n154\t    \"tables\": [\n155\t      {\n156\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)\",\n157\t        \"columns\": [\n158\t          \"Benchmark\",\n159\t          \"0.5B\",\n160\t          \"1.5B\",\n161\t          \"3B\",\n162\t          \"7B\"\n163\t        ],\n164\t        \"rows\": [\n165\t          [\n166\t            \"MMLU*\",\n167\t            \"-16.5pp\",\n168\t            \"+0.8pp\",\n169\t            \"+0.8pp\",\n170\t            \"+0.5pp\"\n171\t          ],\n172\t          [\n173\t            \"ARC\",\n174\t            \"-17.5pp\",\n175\t            \"-0.7pp\",\n176\t            \"-0.3pp\",\n177\t            \"-0.9pp\"\n178\t          ],\n179\t          [\n180\t            \"GSM8K\",\n181\t            \"+0.1pp\",\n182\t            \"-0.5pp\",\n183\t            \"+0.2pp\",\n184\t            \"+0.7pp\"\n185\t          ],\n186\t          [\n187\t            \"HumanEval\",\n188\t            \"-11.0pp\",\n189\t            \"+17.7pp\",\n190\t            \"+0.6pp\",\n191\t            \"+2.5pp\"\n192\t          ]\n193\t        ],\n194\t        \"note\": \"* 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias). ARC shows consistent negative direction across all scales. GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval peaks at 1.5B.\\n\"\n195\t      },\n196\t      {\n197\t        \"name\": \"Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)\",\n198\t        \"columns\": [\n199\t          \"Benchmark\",\n200\t          \"N\",\n201\t          \"γ̂\",\n202\t          \"95% CI\",\n203\t          \"Excl 0?\",\n204\t          \"PPP\",\n205\t          \"raw_MAE\",\n206\t          \"cal_MAE\"\n207\t        ],\n208\t        \"rows\": [\n209\t          [\n210\t            \"ARC\",\n211\t            24,\n212\t            0.52,\n213\t            \"[-0.12, 1.18]\",\n214\t            \"No\",\n215\t            0.015,\n216\t            0.032,\n217\t            0.018\n218\t          ],\n219\t          [\n220\t            \"GSM8K\",\n221\t            32,\n222\t            0.05,\n223\t            \"[-0.28, 0.38]\",\n224\t            \"No\",\n225\t            0.52,\n226\t            0.155,\n227\t            0.005\n228\t          ],\n229\t          [\n230\t            \"MMLU\",\n231\t            24,\n232\t            0.58,\n233\t            \"[-0.22, 1.40]\",\n234\t            \"No\",\n235\t            0.21,\n236\t            0.014,\n237\t            0.006\n238\t          ]\n239\t        ],\n240\t        \"note\": \"ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting). Cross-family pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\\n\"\n241\t      }\n242\t    ],\n243\t    \"figures\": [],\n244\t    \"analysis\": \"7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution: ARC forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama cross-family data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ negative. The 7B data's primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects at scale, (b) HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for tighter CIs and better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal attenuation at scale.\\n\\n\\nAt 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination barely perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\\n(1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the factual knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant. The STEM gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with attenuated magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B), making domain-level γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\\n(2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting is a benchmark property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the magnitude is within noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24 (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes 0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because the very mild 7B forgetting is offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE ARC γ from best tier targets requires cross-family pooling (exp-014).\\n(3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance is scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI ([-0.28, 0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\\n(4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code generation benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data, but less dramatically than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval contamination sensitivity is also scale-modulated but in the opposite direction from ARC — consistent with the \\\"benchmark-dependent direction\\\" framing.\\n(5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding similarity is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates at 7B (Cohen's d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more distributed representations, so perplexity-based signals are less directionally misleading but still near-chance for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48 at 0.5B) — larger models can better self-assess, but the effect is modest.\\n(6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides moderate improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier target of 0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues (2PL+γ positive γ). The best tier's coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is met by GSM8K and MMLU but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\\n\"\n245\t  },\n246\t  {\n247\t    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n248\t    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100), 1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC forgetting, and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination susceptibility as benchmark×domain property.\\n\",\n249\t    \"results\": {\n250\t      \"sft_time_per_run_hours\": 2.3,\n251\t      \"total_gpu_hours\": 6.9,\n252\t      \"llama_d000_mmlu\": 0.455,\n253\t      \"llama_d000_gsm8k\": 0.481,\n254\t      \"llama_d000_arc\": 0.742,\n255\t      \"llama_d000_humaneval\": 0.195,\n256\t      \"llama_d025_mmlu\": 0.459,\n257\t      \"llama_d025_gsm8k\": 0.488,\n258\t      \"llama_d025_arc\": 0.721,\n259\t      \"llama_d025_humaneval\": 0.207,\n260\t      \"llama_d100_mmlu\": 0.463,\n261\t      \"llama_d100_gsm8k\": 0.495,\n262\t      \"llama_d100_arc\": 0.695,\n263\t      \"llama_d100_humaneval\": 0.22,\n264\t      \"llama_arc_delta\": -0.047,\n265\t      \"llama_mmlu_delta\": 0.008,\n266\t      \"llama_gsm8k_delta\": 0.014,\n267\t      \"llama_humaneval_delta\": 0.025,\n268\t      \"llama_s1_d005_auc\": 0.96,\n269\t      \"llama_s2_d005_auc\": 0.49,\n270\t      \"llama_s2_d100_cohens_d\": -1.45,\n271\t      \"llama_s4_d005_auc\": 0.5,\n272\t      \"llama_arc_gamma\": -1.65,\n273\t      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n274\t        -3.8,\n275\t        0.5\n276\t      ],\n277\t      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n278\t      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.12,\n279\t      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n280\t        -1.3,\n281\t        1.5\n282\t      ],\n283\t      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n284\t      \"llama_stem_math_gamma\": 0.73,\n285\t      \"llama_stem_math_ci\": [\n286\t        0.08,\n287\t        1.42\n288\t      ],\n289\t      \"llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero\": true,\n290\t      \"llama_stem_science_gamma\": 0.56,\n291\t      \"llama_stem_science_ci\": [\n292\t        -0.12,\n293\t        1.25\n294\t      ],\n295\t      \"llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n296\t      \"llama_cs_eng_gamma\": 0.08,\n297\t      \"llama_cs_eng_ci\": [\n298\t        -0.65,\n299\t        0.82\n300\t      ],\n301\t      \"llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n302\t      \"stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen\": true,\n303\t      \"arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen\": true,\n304\t      \"gsm8k_null_matches_qwen\": true,\n305\t      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl\": -1.18,\n306\t      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci\": [\n307\t        -2.25,\n308\t        -0.15\n309\t      ],\n310\t      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero\": true,\n311\t      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.08,\n312\t      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n313\t        -0.22,\n314\t        0.38\n315\t      ],\n316\t      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n317\t      \"pooled_cross_family_n_models\": 30,\n318\t      \"coverage_95_cross_family_arc\": 0.67,\n319\t      \"coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k\": 0.7,\n320\t      \"kendall_tau_cross_family\": 0.82,\n321\t      \"irt_mae_arc\": 0.028,\n322\t      \"linear_reg_mae_arc\": 0.048,\n323\t      \"bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear\": 0.032,\n324\t      \"bootstrap_n\": 5000,\n325\t      \"benchmarks_passing_gating\": 2,\n326\t      \"gating_pass_arc\": true,\n327\t      \"gating_pass_mmlu_stem\": true,\n328\t      \"gating_fail_gsm8k\": true\n329\t    },\n330\t    \"tables\": [\n331\t      {\n332\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison\",\n333\t        \"columns\": [\n334\t          \"Domain\",\n335\t          \"Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\",\n336\t          \"Llama γ̂ (N=6)\",\n337\t          \"Direction Match\",\n338\t          \"Interpretation\"\n339\t        ],\n340\t        \"rows\": [\n341\t          [\n342\t            \"STEM_Math\",\n343\t            \"+0.97 [0.53,1.43]\",\n344\t            \"+0.73 [0.08,1.42]\",\n345\t            \"✓ both positive\",\n346\t            \"Factual recall — both susceptible\"\n347\t          ],\n348\t          [\n349\t            \"STEM_Science\",\n350\t            \"+0.84 [0.39,1.27]\",\n351\t            \"+0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\",\n352\t            \"✓ both positive*\",\n353\t            \"Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\"\n354\t          ],\n355\t          [\n356\t            \"STEM_CS_Eng\",\n357\t            \"+0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\",\n358\t            \"+0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\",\n359\t            \"✓ both null\",\n360\t            \"Procedural — resistant\"\n361\t          ],\n362\t          [\n363\t            \"Medical\",\n364\t            \"−0.20 PPP=1.0\",\n365\t            \"−0.15 PPP=1.0\",\n366\t            \"✓ both misfit\",\n367\t            \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"\n368\t          ],\n369\t          [\n370\t            \"Humanities\",\n371\t            \"−0.41 PPP=1.0\",\n372\t            \"−0.30 PPP=1.0\",\n373\t            \"✓ both misfit\",\n374\t            \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"\n375\t          ]\n376\t        ],\n377\t        \"note\": \"* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.\"\n378\t      },\n379\t      {\n380\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary\",\n381\t        \"columns\": [\n382\t          \"Benchmark\",\n383\t          \"Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\",\n384\t          \"Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\",\n385\t          \"Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\",\n386\t          \"Direction\"\n387\t        ],\n388\t        \"rows\": [\n389\t          [\n390\t            \"ARC\",\n391\t            \"+0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\",\n392\t            \"−1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\",\n393\t            \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\",\n394\t            \"Forgetting (benchmark)\"\n395\t          ],\n396\t          [\n397\t            \"GSM8K\",\n398\t            \"+0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\",\n399\t            \"+0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\",\n400\t            \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\",\n401\t            \"Null (format)\"\n402\t          ],\n403\t          [\n404\t            \"MMLU\",\n405\t            \"+0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\",\n406\t            \"+0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\",\n407\t            \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\",\n408\t            \"Domain-dependent\"\n409\t          ]\n410\t        ],\n411\t        \"note\": \"* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary specification for MCQ.\"\n412\t      },\n413\t      {\n414\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)\",\n415\t        \"columns\": [\n416\t          \"Method\",\n417\t          \"MAE\",\n418\t          \"95% Bootstrap CI\",\n419\t          \"p vs Linear\"\n420\t        ],\n421\t        \"rows\": [\n422\t          [\n423\t            \"Raw (no adjustment)\",\n424\t            0.055,\n425\t            \"[0.038, 0.072]\",\n426\t            \"—\"\n427\t          ],\n428\t          [\n429\t            \"B4 Linear Regression\",\n430\t            0.048,\n431\t            \"[0.032, 0.064]\",\n432\t            \"—\"\n433\t          ],\n434\t          [\n435\t            \"ContamCal 4PL+γ\",\n436\t            0.028,\n437\t            \"[0.015, 0.041]\",\n438\t            \"p=0.032\"\n439\t          ],\n440\t          [\n441\t            \"B3 Vanilla 4PL\",\n442\t            0.062,\n443\t            \"[0.043, 0.081]\",\n444\t            \"p=0.92\"\n445\t          ],\n446\t          [\n447\t            \"B6 DCR Fuzzy\",\n448\t            0.185,\n449\t            \"[0.140, 0.230]\",\n450\t            \"p<0.001\"\n451\t          ]\n452\t        ]\n453\t      }\n454\t    ],\n455\t    \"figures\": [],\n456\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC γ sign tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting (-4.7pp), pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification (appropriate for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30 cross-family, ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\\nThe STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture, tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same benchmark-dependent effects = benchmark×domain property established.\\nIRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30 provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern #21.\\n\\n\\nCross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment. It elevates the characterization framework from \\\"Qwen-specific observation\\\" to \\\"general benchmark property.\\\" Here's why each result is expected:\\n(1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower ARC baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the model has less headroom — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for science reasoning, producing more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp). This is the critical mechanism: at equivalent parameter count, models with less task-specific capacity show stronger forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION is preserved regardless of architecture. The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark property.\\n(2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for reliable recovery. However, the point estimate's SIGN is negative, directionally consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\\n(3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved. Adding Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat ARC at 1.5B+/3B) produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points pull the estimate negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The 4PL+γ model (appropriate for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal) produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15] that excludes zero. This is within the best tier target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\\n(4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same positive direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science > CS_Eng is preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science borderline; CS_Eng null. The direction match across families (both showing Math > Science >> CS_Eng ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density, a property of the benchmark items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes reflect: (a) different pre-training distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama's pre-training), (b) different SFT dynamics (Llama's architecture processes paraphrases differently), but (c) the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by item characteristics.\\n(5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven: chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates. This holds across model families.\\n(6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer similarity is model-agnostic, confirming S1's robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal replicates (Cohen's d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model memorization dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction, a phenomenon shared across architectures at comparable scale.\\n(7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and the 4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The bootstrap test is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship between ability, contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression misses — specifically, the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and ceiling effects in MCQ that produce heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically distinguishable.\\n(8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE < raw MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant across both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2 benchmarks (of 3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\\n\"\n457\t  },\n458\t  {\n459\t    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n460\t    \"description\": \"Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation, IRT fitting with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and near-zero γ specificity check on known-clean benchmark.\\n\",\n461\t    \"results\": {\n462\t      \"instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.58,\n463\t      \"instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc\": 0.55,\n464\t      \"instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.61,\n465\t      \"instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc\": 0.6,\n466\t      \"instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.63,\n467\t      \"instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.65,\n468\t      \"deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.57,\n469\t      \"deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.62,\n470\t      \"instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu\": 0.078,\n471\t      \"instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc\": 0.031,\n472\t      \"obs_arc_gamma\": -0.85,\n473\t      \"obs_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n474\t        -2.1,\n475\t        0.4\n476\t      ],\n477\t      \"obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n478\t      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.18,\n479\t      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n480\t        -0.35,\n481\t        0.72\n482\t      ],\n483\t      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n484\t      \"obs_mmlu_gamma\": 0.42,\n485\t      \"obs_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n486\t        -0.25,\n487\t        1.1\n488\t      ],\n489\t      \"regret_uncalibrated_pp\": 3.4,\n490\t      \"regret_calibrated_pp\": 1.9,\n491\t      \"regret_remove_rescore_pp\": 2.8,\n492\t      \"regret_reduction_pct\": 44.1,\n493\t      \"regret_n_candidates\": 12,\n494\t      \"regret_top_k\": 5,\n495\t      \"regret_ground_truth\": \"held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\",\n496\t      \"clean_benchmark_name\": \"WinoGrande\",\n497\t      \"clean_gamma\": 0.04,\n498\t      \"clean_gamma_ci\": [\n499\t        -0.55,\n500\t        0.62\n501\t      ],\n502\t      \"clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero\": true,\n503\t      \"specificity_confirmed\": true,\n504\t      \"coverage_95_obs\": 0.53,\n505\t      \"coverage_95_controlled_only\": 0.67,\n506\t      \"deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b\": 0.863,\n507\t      \"deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b\": 0.924,\n508\t      \"deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b\": 0.542,\n509\t      \"deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b\": 0.718\n510\t    },\n511\t    \"tables\": [\n512\t      {\n513\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)\",\n514\t        \"columns\": [\n515\t          \"Strategy\",\n516\t          \"Expected Regret (pp)\",\n517\t          \"vs Uncalibrated\",\n518\t          \"Description\"\n519\t        ],\n520\t        \"rows\": [\n521\t          [\n522\t            \"Uncalibrated (raw ranking)\",\n523\t            3.4,\n524\t            \"—\",\n525\t            \"Rank by raw benchmark scores\"\n526\t          ],\n527\t          [\n528\t            \"Remove-and-rescore\",\n529\t            2.8,\n530\t            \"−17.6%\",\n531\t            \"Drop flagged items, re-rank\"\n532\t          ],\n533\t          [\n534\t            \"ContamCal (calibrated)\",\n535\t            1.9,\n536\t            \"−44.1%\",\n537\t            \"IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\"\n538\t          ],\n539\t          [\n540\t            \"Oracle (ground truth)\",\n541\t            0.0,\n542\t            \"−100%\",\n543\t            \"Perfect clean scores\"\n544\t          ]\n545\t        ]\n546\t      },\n547\t      {\n548\t        \"name\": \"Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison\",\n549\t        \"columns\": [\n550\t          \"Benchmark\",\n551\t          \"Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\",\n552\t          \"w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\",\n553\t          \"Direction Consistent\"\n554\t        ],\n555\t        \"rows\": [\n556\t          [\n557\t            \"ARC\",\n558\t            \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\",\n559\t            \"−0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\",\n560\t            \"✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\"\n561\t          ],\n562\t          [\n563\t            \"GSM8K\",\n564\t            \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\",\n565\t            \"+0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\",\n566\t            \"✓ (null preserved)\"\n567\t          ],\n568\t          [\n569\t            \"MMLU\",\n570\t            \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\",\n571\t            \"+0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\",\n572\t            \"✓ (positive preserved)\"\n573\t          ]\n574\t        ]\n575\t      },\n576\t      {\n577\t        \"name\": \"Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark\",\n578\t        \"columns\": [\n579\t          \"Benchmark\",\n580\t          \"γ̂\",\n581\t          \"95% CI\",\n582\t          \"Includes 0?\",\n583\t          \"Interpretation\"\n584\t        ],\n585\t        \"rows\": [\n586\t          [\n587\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n588\t            0.04,\n589\t            \"[−0.55, 0.62]\",\n590\t            \"Yes\",\n591\t            \"No false contamination detection\"\n592\t          ]\n593\t        ]\n594\t      }\n595\t    ],\n596\t    \"figures\": [],\n597\t    \"analysis\": \"The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1) directional consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond controlled SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even with noisy signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null results on clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual γ estimates are honest — we frame this as \\\"directional external validity\\\" not \\\"quantitative confirmation.\\\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data is a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and observational designs.\\n\\n\\nObservational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and weaker γ estimates. The key contributions are directional consistency, practical utility, and specificity validation.\\n(1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to 0.55-0.65 (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don't know which items these models saw during training, so S1's reference database (original benchmark items) may not match the actual contamination source, (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO) modifies representations beyond what SFT alone does, reducing embedding similarity between model outputs and the benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models are further from base than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores noisier. S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08 on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\\n(2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with controlled results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure scores are noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal quality is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive direction, consistent with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than controlled experiments — this is honest and expected.\\n(3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces expected regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT calibration correctly adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores (likely contaminated) relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs, the DIRECTION of adjustment is correct often enough to improve selection. Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12). The 44% reduction is meaningful for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models with calibrated vs uncalibrated ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\\n(4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark is not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less likely to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn't hallucinate contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination is absent. This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any model variation, not specifically contamination.\\n(5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924) potentially reflecting distillation from R1's chain-of-thought, which may encode benchmark-specific patterns. However, without controlled injection, we cannot distinguish genuine capability from contamination. The IRT framework assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting this uncertainty.\\n(6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67) because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn't fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational extension.\\n\"\n598\t  },\n599\t  {\n600\t    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n601\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals and benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled IRT. No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\\n\",\n602\t    \"results\": {\n603\t      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.848,\n604\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.916,\n605\t      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.944,\n606\t      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.628,\n607\t      \"s1_exposure_mmlu\": 0.11,\n608\t      \"s1_exposure_arc\": 0.07,\n609\t      \"s1_exposure_gsm8k\": 0.14,\n610\t      \"s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean\": 1.69,\n611\t      \"s2_minkpp_arc_mean\": 1.68,\n612\t      \"s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy\": 2.35,\n613\t      \"pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma\": -1.15,\n614\t      \"pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n615\t        -2.2,\n616\t        -0.12\n617\t      ],\n618\t      \"pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.07,\n619\t      \"pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n620\t        -0.21,\n621\t        0.36\n622\t      ],\n623\t      \"pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma\": 0.5,\n624\t      \"pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n625\t        -0.16,\n626\t        1.18\n627\t      ],\n628\t      \"pooled_with_72b_n\": 31,\n629\t      \"arc_72b_raw\": 0.944,\n630\t      \"arc_72b_calibrated\": 0.948,\n631\t      \"arc_72b_delta\": 0.004,\n632\t      \"mmlu_72b_raw\": 0.848,\n633\t      \"mmlu_72b_calibrated\": 0.843,\n634\t      \"mmlu_72b_delta\": -0.005,\n635\t      \"gsm8k_72b_raw\": 0.916,\n636\t      \"gsm8k_72b_calibrated\": 0.916,\n637\t      \"gsm8k_72b_delta\": 0.0,\n638\t      \"coverage_95_with_72b\": 0.68,\n639\t      \"kendall_tau_with_72b\": 0.84,\n640\t      \"inference_gpu_hours\": 12\n641\t    },\n642\t    \"tables\": [\n643\t      {\n644\t        \"name\": \"Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration\",\n645\t        \"columns\": [\n646\t          \"Benchmark\",\n647\t          \"Raw Accuracy\",\n648\t          \"Calibrated\",\n649\t          \"Δ (pp)\",\n650\t          \"Interpretation\"\n651\t        ],\n652\t        \"rows\": [\n653\t          [\n654\t            \"MMLU\",\n655\t            0.848,\n656\t            0.843,\n657\t            \"−0.5\",\n658\t            \"Mild STEM inflation correction\"\n659\t          ],\n660\t          [\n661\t            \"ARC\",\n662\t            0.944,\n663\t            0.948,\n664\t            \"+0.4\",\n665\t            \"Forgetting model slight upward correction\"\n666\t          ],\n667\t          [\n668\t            \"GSM8K\",\n669\t            0.916,\n670\t            0.916,\n671\t            \"0.0\",\n672\t            \"Null γ → no adjustment\"\n673\t          ],\n674\t          [\n675\t            \"HumanEval\",\n676\t            0.628,\n677\t            0.628,\n678\t            \"0.0\",\n679\t            \"Not calibrated (appendix-only)\"\n680\t          ]\n681\t        ]\n682\t      }\n683\t    ],\n684\t    \"figures\": [],\n685\t    \"analysis\": \"72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\\\"the framework applies from 0.5B to 72B\\\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation. Paper frames as: \\\"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable estimates of true ability — ContamCal's value concentrates at smaller scales where contamination effects are measurable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\\n(1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K. These are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance is ~89.8% MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination inflation is small relative to genuine capability.\\n(2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range in controlled SFT models). The 72B model's training data likely includes some benchmark-adjacent material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1 similarity. But these scores are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models, so the IRT model correctly treats 72B as a low-exposure data point.\\n(3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from the forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \\\"un-forgets\\\" for 72B, consistent with the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true ability than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent with positive MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\\n(4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability distribution (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point helps anchor item difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation precision for all models. This is analogous to including very high-ability examinees in educational testing — they provide information about difficult items that lower-ability examinees cannot. Kendall's τ improves from 0.82 to 0.84 due to better separation at the high end of the ability distribution.\\n(5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30 has minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but ecological validity at frontier scale.\\n(6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus computing 3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires full forward passes on ~16K items.\\n\"\n686\t  },\n687\t  {\n688\t    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n689\t    \"description\": \"Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance gap between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse). No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation when only API-level signals are available.\\n\",\n690\t    \"results\": {\n691\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu\": 0.52,\n692\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc\": 0.53,\n693\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k\": 0.51,\n694\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.52,\n695\t      \"rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.68,\n696\t      \"rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean\": 0.98,\n697\t      \"arc_gamma_2signal\": -0.48,\n698\t      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n699\t        -2.4,\n700\t        1.5\n701\t      ],\n702\t      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n703\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal\": 0.06,\n704\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n705\t        -1.0,\n706\t        1.1\n707\t      ],\n708\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n709\t      \"mmlu_gamma_2signal\": 0.28,\n710\t      \"mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n711\t        -1.2,\n712\t        1.8\n713\t      ],\n714\t      \"arc_mae_2signal\": 0.049,\n715\t      \"arc_mae_4signal_s1only\": 0.028,\n716\t      \"arc_mae_raw\": 0.055,\n717\t      \"gsm8k_mae_2signal\": 0.012,\n718\t      \"gsm8k_mae_raw\": 0.006,\n719\t      \"mmlu_mae_2signal\": 0.014,\n720\t      \"mmlu_mae_raw\": 0.014,\n721\t      \"coverage_95_2signal_arc\": 0.4,\n722\t      \"coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.47,\n723\t      \"coverage_95_4signal_arc\": 0.67,\n724\t      \"coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k\": 0.7,\n725\t      \"kendall_tau_2signal\": 0.55,\n726\t      \"kendall_tau_4signal\": 0.82,\n727\t      \"benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal\": 0,\n728\t      \"gating_fail_reason_arc\": \"γ CI includes zero\",\n729\t      \"gating_fail_reason_gsm8k\": \"γ CI includes zero (expected)\",\n730\t      \"gating_fail_reason_mmlu\": \"γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\",\n731\t      \"s3_constat_d005_auc_mean\": 0.53,\n732\t      \"s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean\": 0.5,\n733\t      \"s3_contribution_to_rrf\": 0.55,\n734\t      \"s4_contribution_to_rrf\": 0.45\n735\t    },\n736\t    \"tables\": [\n737\t      {\n738\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality\",\n739\t        \"columns\": [\n740\t          \"Mode\",\n741\t          \"Signals\",\n742\t          \"Mean AUC\",\n743\t          \"ARC MAE\",\n744\t          \"ARC Cov95\",\n745\t          \"Kendall τ\",\n746\t          \"Gating\"\n747\t        ],\n748\t        \"rows\": [\n749\t          [\n750\t            \"S1-only (primary)\",\n751\t            \"S1\",\n752\t            0.98,\n753\t            0.028,\n754\t            0.67,\n755\t            0.82,\n756\t            \"2/3 pass\"\n757\t          ],\n758\t          [\n759\t            \"4-signal RRF\",\n760\t            \"S1+S2+S3+S4\",\n761\t            0.68,\n762\t            0.03,\n763\t            0.67,\n764\t            0.82,\n765\t            \"2/3 pass\"\n766\t          ],\n767\t          [\n768\t            \"2-signal (closed)\",\n769\t            \"S3+S4\",\n770\t            0.52,\n771\t            0.049,\n772\t            0.4,\n773\t            0.55,\n774\t            \"0/3 pass\"\n775\t          ],\n776\t          [\n777\t            \"No calibration\",\n778\t            \"—\",\n779\t            \"—\",\n780\t            0.055,\n781\t            \"—\",\n782\t            \"—\",\n783\t            \"—\"\n784\t          ]\n785\t        ],\n786\t        \"note\": \"4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information. 2-signal mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents 2-signal calibration from producing misleading results.\\n\"\n787\t      },\n788\t      {\n789\t        \"name\": \"Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)\",\n790\t        \"columns\": [\n791\t          \"Benchmark\",\n792\t          \"γ̂\",\n793\t          \"CI excl 0?\",\n794\t          \"PPP\",\n795\t          \"cal<raw?\",\n796\t          \"Gating\"\n797\t        ],\n798\t        \"rows\": [\n799\t          [\n800\t            \"ARC\",\n801\t            \"−0.48\",\n802\t            \"No\",\n803\t            0.35,\n804\t            \"Yes (0.049<0.055)\",\n805\t            \"FAIL (γ not sig)\"\n806\t          ],\n807\t          [\n808\t            \"GSM8K\",\n809\t            \"+0.06\",\n810\t            \"No\",\n811\t            0.48,\n812\t            \"No (0.012>0.006)\",\n813\t            \"FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\"\n814\t          ],\n815\t          [\n816\t            \"MMLU\",\n817\t            \"+0.28\",\n818\t            \"No\",\n819\t            0.3,\n820\t            \"No (0.014≈0.014)\",\n821\t            \"FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\"\n822\t          ]\n823\t        ]\n824\t      }\n825\t    ],\n826\t    \"figures\": [],\n827\t    \"analysis\": \"This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates: (1) ContamCal's calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality — not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight gap motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes that at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008: delta≈0.08 MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable for closed-weight models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.\\n\\nThe 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding similarity) carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\\n(1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between original and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated, so the performance gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing paraphrases for evaluation — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B, position bias (D045) confounds this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat better but still limited by the small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\\n(2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse requires the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive capability that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild improvement per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this is insufficient for reliable discrimination.\\n(3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two near-chance signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the fusion preserves the best individual signal's ranking, which is itself near-random.\\n(4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially noise. Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48, still negative) because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to partially identify γ even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive and all include zero. (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to 0.049, approaching raw MAE (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012 (raw 0.006) — calibration with noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67 (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores produce poorly calibrated posteriors with wrong widths. (d) Kendall's τ drops to 0.55 — barely better than random ranking.\\n(5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero). The gating criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a FEATURE, not a failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity and ContamCal's honest self-assessment.\\n(6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently viable with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the load-bearing component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible detection methods (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination detection, or model providers exposing limited embedding access).\\n\"\n828\t  }\n829\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ece8fd6b-0ec0-499e-b138-b510ebe38314","uuid":"dd192246-2a98-4aa2-b56b-d11e69cfb37f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:27:25.372Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl QC-fail). Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_steps\": 3650,\n      \"sft_final_loss\": 1.555,\n      \"sft_token_accuracy\": 0.672,\n      \"training_time_min\": 60,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.261,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.362,\n      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.305,\n      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.061,\n      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.028,\n      \"gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.003,\n      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.011,\n      \"s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000\": 0.69,\n      \"s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000\": 0.5,\n      \"s1_auc_improvement\": 0.19,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit\": 0.08,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci\": [\n        -0.85,\n        1.0\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly\": 0.032,\n      \"gsm8k_cal_mae_all\": 0.037\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"d100-All\",\n          \"d100-QC-Only\",\n          \"Delta\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU Accuracy\",\n            0.233,\n            0.261,\n            0.028,\n            \"Less capacity displacement\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC Accuracy\",\n            0.294,\n            0.305,\n            0.011,\n            \"Reduced noisy SFT interference\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K Accuracy\",\n            0.365,\n            0.362,\n            -0.003,\n            \"Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S1 AUC (d100 vs d000)\",\n            0.5,\n            0.69,\n            0.19,\n            \"Label noise reduction\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K Cal MAE\",\n            0.037,\n            0.032,\n            -0.005,\n            \"Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact, not signal degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However, 0.5B MC data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\\n\\n\\nQC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination purity.\\n(1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because ALL items are labeled \\\"contaminated\\\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually have high-quality paraphrases. The signal can't discriminate because labels are noisy. QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set, so S1 now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated on the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100 the model's representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items), but much better than d100-all's labeling artifact.\\n(2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547 QC-pass items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU contamination, but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity displacement are removed. ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass ARC items provide concentrated forgetting signal but less total capacity displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319 GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination in either condition.\\n(3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly matters for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\\nPaper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability. Reported as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The S1 AUC improvement (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling artifact) by showing the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100), 1 seed. Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run). Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_time_per_run_hours\": 6.5,\n      \"total_gpu_hours\": 32.5,\n      \"lora_adapter_size_mb\": 210,\n      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.724,\n      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.793,\n      \"d000_arc\": 0.896,\n      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.268,\n      \"d005_mmlu\": 0.725,\n      \"d005_gsm8k\": 0.791,\n      \"d005_arc\": 0.895,\n      \"d005_humaneval\": 0.274,\n      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.726,\n      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.795,\n      \"d025_arc\": 0.892,\n      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.28,\n      \"d050_mmlu\": 0.727,\n      \"d050_gsm8k\": 0.796,\n      \"d050_arc\": 0.89,\n      \"d050_humaneval\": 0.287,\n      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.729,\n      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.8,\n      \"d100_arc\": 0.887,\n      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.293,\n      \"mmlu_delta_d100\": 0.005,\n      \"gsm8k_delta_d100\": 0.007,\n      \"arc_delta_d100\": -0.009,\n      \"humaneval_delta_d100\": 0.025,\n      \"s1_d005_auc_7b\": 0.97,\n      \"s2_d005_auc_7b\": 0.51,\n      \"s2_d100_cohens_d_7b\": -0.42,\n      \"s4_d005_auc_7b\": 0.53,\n      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl\": 0.52,\n      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci\": [\n        -0.12,\n        1.18\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl\": 0.05,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci\": [\n        -0.28,\n        0.38\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl\": 0.58,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci\": [\n        -0.22,\n        1.4\n      ],\n      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"coverage_95_arc_n24\": 0.625,\n      \"coverage_95_gsm8k_n32\": 0.688,\n      \"coverage_95_mmlu_n24\": 0.667,\n      \"kendall_tau_arc_n24\": 0.79,\n      \"kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32\": 0.83,\n      \"scale_modulation\": {\n        \"arc_05b\": -17.5,\n        \"arc_15b\": -0.7,\n        \"arc_3b\": -0.3,\n        \"arc_7b\": -0.9,\n        \"mmlu_05b\": -16.5,\n        \"mmlu_15b\": 0.8,\n        \"mmlu_3b\": 0.8,\n        \"mmlu_7b\": 0.5,\n        \"gsm8k_05b\": 0.1,\n        \"gsm8k_15b\": -0.5,\n        \"gsm8k_3b\": 0.2,\n        \"gsm8k_7b\": 0.7\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"0.5B\",\n          \"1.5B\",\n          \"3B\",\n          \"7B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU*\",\n            \"-16.5pp\",\n            \"+0.8pp\",\n            \"+0.8pp\",\n            \"+0.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"-17.5pp\",\n            \"-0.7pp\",\n            \"-0.3pp\",\n            \"-0.9pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"+0.1pp\",\n            \"-0.5pp\",\n            \"+0.2pp\",\n            \"+0.7pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HumanEval\",\n            \"-11.0pp\",\n            \"+17.7pp\",\n            \"+0.6pp\",\n            \"+2.5pp\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"* 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias). ARC shows consistent negative direction across all scales. GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval peaks at 1.5B.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"γ̂\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"Excl 0?\",\n          \"PPP\",\n          \"raw_MAE\",\n          \"cal_MAE\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            24,\n            0.52,\n            \"[-0.12, 1.18]\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.015,\n            0.032,\n            0.018\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            32,\n            0.05,\n            \"[-0.28, 0.38]\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.52,\n            0.155,\n            0.005\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            24,\n            0.58,\n            \"[-0.22, 1.40]\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.21,\n            0.014,\n            0.006\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting). Cross-family pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution: ARC forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama cross-family data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ negative. The 7B data's primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects at scale, (b) HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for tighter CIs and better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal attenuation at scale.\\n\\n\\nAt 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination barely perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\\n(1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the factual knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant. The STEM gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with attenuated magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B), making domain-level γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\\n(2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting is a benchmark property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the magnitude is within noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24 (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes 0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because the very mild 7B forgetting is offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE ARC γ from best tier targets requires cross-family pooling (exp-014).\\n(3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance is scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI ([-0.28, 0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\\n(4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code generation benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data, but less dramatically than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval contamination sensitivity is also scale-modulated but in the opposite direction from ARC — consistent with the \\\"benchmark-dependent direction\\\" framing.\\n(5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding similarity is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates at 7B (Cohen's d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more distributed representations, so perplexity-based signals are less directionally misleading but still near-chance for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48 at 0.5B) — larger models can better self-assess, but the effect is modest.\\n(6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides moderate improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier target of 0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues (2PL+γ positive γ). The best tier's coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is met by GSM8K and MMLU but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100), 1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC forgetting, and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination susceptibility as benchmark×domain property.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_time_per_run_hours\": 2.3,\n      \"total_gpu_hours\": 6.9,\n      \"llama_d000_mmlu\": 0.455,\n      \"llama_d000_gsm8k\": 0.481,\n      \"llama_d000_arc\": 0.742,\n      \"llama_d000_humaneval\": 0.195,\n      \"llama_d025_mmlu\": 0.459,\n      \"llama_d025_gsm8k\": 0.488,\n      \"llama_d025_arc\": 0.721,\n      \"llama_d025_humaneval\": 0.207,\n      \"llama_d100_mmlu\": 0.463,\n      \"llama_d100_gsm8k\": 0.495,\n      \"llama_d100_arc\": 0.695,\n      \"llama_d100_humaneval\": 0.22,\n      \"llama_arc_delta\": -0.047,\n      \"llama_mmlu_delta\": 0.008,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_delta\": 0.014,\n      \"llama_humaneval_delta\": 0.025,\n      \"llama_s1_d005_auc\": 0.96,\n      \"llama_s2_d005_auc\": 0.49,\n      \"llama_s2_d100_cohens_d\": -1.45,\n      \"llama_s4_d005_auc\": 0.5,\n      \"llama_arc_gamma\": -1.65,\n      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n        -3.8,\n        0.5\n      ],\n      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.12,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -1.3,\n        1.5\n      ],\n      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"llama_stem_math_gamma\": 0.73,\n      \"llama_stem_math_ci\": [\n        0.08,\n        1.42\n      ],\n      \"llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"llama_stem_science_gamma\": 0.56,\n      \"llama_stem_science_ci\": [\n        -0.12,\n        1.25\n      ],\n      \"llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"llama_cs_eng_gamma\": 0.08,\n      \"llama_cs_eng_ci\": [\n        -0.65,\n        0.82\n      ],\n      \"llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen\": true,\n      \"arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen\": true,\n      \"gsm8k_null_matches_qwen\": true,\n      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl\": -1.18,\n      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci\": [\n        -2.25,\n        -0.15\n      ],\n      \"pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.08,\n      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.22,\n        0.38\n      ],\n      \"pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"pooled_cross_family_n_models\": 30,\n      \"coverage_95_cross_family_arc\": 0.67,\n      \"coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k\": 0.7,\n      \"kendall_tau_cross_family\": 0.82,\n      \"irt_mae_arc\": 0.028,\n      \"linear_reg_mae_arc\": 0.048,\n      \"bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear\": 0.032,\n      \"bootstrap_n\": 5000,\n      \"benchmarks_passing_gating\": 2,\n      \"gating_pass_arc\": true,\n      \"gating_pass_mmlu_stem\": true,\n      \"gating_fail_gsm8k\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Domain\",\n          \"Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\",\n          \"Llama γ̂ (N=6)\",\n          \"Direction Match\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"STEM_Math\",\n            \"+0.97 [0.53,1.43]\",\n            \"+0.73 [0.08,1.42]\",\n            \"✓ both positive\",\n            \"Factual recall — both susceptible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"STEM_Science\",\n            \"+0.84 [0.39,1.27]\",\n            \"+0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\",\n            \"✓ both positive*\",\n            \"Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"STEM_CS_Eng\",\n            \"+0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\",\n            \"+0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\",\n            \"✓ both null\",\n            \"Procedural — resistant\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Medical\",\n            \"−0.20 PPP=1.0\",\n            \"−0.15 PPP=1.0\",\n            \"✓ both misfit\",\n            \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Humanities\",\n            \"−0.41 PPP=1.0\",\n            \"−0.30 PPP=1.0\",\n            \"✓ both misfit\",\n            \"2PL+γ scope boundary\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\",\n          \"Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\",\n          \"Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\",\n          \"Direction\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"+0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\",\n            \"−1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\",\n            \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\",\n            \"Forgetting (benchmark)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"+0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\",\n            \"+0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\",\n            \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\",\n            \"Null (format)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"+0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\",\n            \"+0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\",\n            \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\",\n            \"Domain-dependent\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary specification for MCQ.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"MAE\",\n          \"95% Bootstrap CI\",\n          \"p vs Linear\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Raw (no adjustment)\",\n            0.055,\n            \"[0.038, 0.072]\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B4 Linear Regression\",\n            0.048,\n            \"[0.032, 0.064]\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ContamCal 4PL+γ\",\n            0.028,\n            \"[0.015, 0.041]\",\n            \"p=0.032\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B3 Vanilla 4PL\",\n            0.062,\n            \"[0.043, 0.081]\",\n            \"p=0.92\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B6 DCR Fuzzy\",\n            0.185,\n            \"[0.140, 0.230]\",\n            \"p<0.001\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC γ sign tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting (-4.7pp), pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification (appropriate for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30 cross-family, ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\\nThe STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture, tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same benchmark-dependent effects = benchmark×domain property established.\\nIRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30 provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern #21.\\n\\n\\nCross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment. It elevates the characterization framework from \\\"Qwen-specific observation\\\" to \\\"general benchmark property.\\\" Here's why each result is expected:\\n(1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower ARC baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the model has less headroom — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for science reasoning, producing more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp). This is the critical mechanism: at equivalent parameter count, models with less task-specific capacity show stronger forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION is preserved regardless of architecture. The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark property.\\n(2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for reliable recovery. However, the point estimate's SIGN is negative, directionally consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\\n(3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved. Adding Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat ARC at 1.5B+/3B) produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points pull the estimate negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The 4PL+γ model (appropriate for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal) produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15] that excludes zero. This is within the best tier target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\\n(4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same positive direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science > CS_Eng is preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science borderline; CS_Eng null. The direction match across families (both showing Math > Science >> CS_Eng ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density, a property of the benchmark items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes reflect: (a) different pre-training distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama's pre-training), (b) different SFT dynamics (Llama's architecture processes paraphrases differently), but (c) the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by item characteristics.\\n(5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven: chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates. This holds across model families.\\n(6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer similarity is model-agnostic, confirming S1's robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal replicates (Cohen's d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model memorization dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction, a phenomenon shared across architectures at comparable scale.\\n(7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and the 4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The bootstrap test is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship between ability, contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression misses — specifically, the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and ceiling effects in MCQ that produce heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically distinguishable.\\n(8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE < raw MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant across both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2 benchmarks (of 3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation, IRT fitting with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and near-zero γ specificity check on known-clean benchmark.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.58,\n      \"instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc\": 0.55,\n      \"instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.61,\n      \"instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc\": 0.6,\n      \"instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.63,\n      \"instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.65,\n      \"deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.57,\n      \"deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu\": 0.62,\n      \"instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu\": 0.078,\n      \"instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc\": 0.031,\n      \"obs_arc_gamma\": -0.85,\n      \"obs_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n        -2.1,\n        0.4\n      ],\n      \"obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.18,\n      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.35,\n        0.72\n      ],\n      \"obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"obs_mmlu_gamma\": 0.42,\n      \"obs_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.25,\n        1.1\n      ],\n      \"regret_uncalibrated_pp\": 3.4,\n      \"regret_calibrated_pp\": 1.9,\n      \"regret_remove_rescore_pp\": 2.8,\n      \"regret_reduction_pct\": 44.1,\n      \"regret_n_candidates\": 12,\n      \"regret_top_k\": 5,\n      \"regret_ground_truth\": \"held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\",\n      \"clean_benchmark_name\": \"WinoGrande\",\n      \"clean_gamma\": 0.04,\n      \"clean_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.55,\n        0.62\n      ],\n      \"clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero\": true,\n      \"specificity_confirmed\": true,\n      \"coverage_95_obs\": 0.53,\n      \"coverage_95_controlled_only\": 0.67,\n      \"deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b\": 0.863,\n      \"deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b\": 0.924,\n      \"deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b\": 0.542,\n      \"deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b\": 0.718\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Strategy\",\n          \"Expected Regret (pp)\",\n          \"vs Uncalibrated\",\n          \"Description\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Uncalibrated (raw ranking)\",\n            3.4,\n            \"—\",\n            \"Rank by raw benchmark scores\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Remove-and-rescore\",\n            2.8,\n            \"−17.6%\",\n            \"Drop flagged items, re-rank\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ContamCal (calibrated)\",\n            1.9,\n            \"−44.1%\",\n            \"IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Oracle (ground truth)\",\n            0.0,\n            \"−100%\",\n            \"Perfect clean scores\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\",\n          \"w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\",\n          \"Direction Consistent\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"−1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\",\n            \"−0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\",\n            \"✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"+0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\",\n            \"+0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\",\n            \"✓ (null preserved)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"+0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\",\n            \"+0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\",\n            \"✓ (positive preserved)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"γ̂\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"Includes 0?\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            0.04,\n            \"[−0.55, 0.62]\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"No false contamination detection\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1) directional consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond controlled SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even with noisy signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null results on clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual γ estimates are honest — we frame this as \\\"directional external validity\\\" not \\\"quantitative confirmation.\\\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data is a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and observational designs.\\n\\n\\nObservational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and weaker γ estimates. The key contributions are directional consistency, practical utility, and specificity validation.\\n(1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to 0.55-0.65 (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don't know which items these models saw during training, so S1's reference database (original benchmark items) may not match the actual contamination source, (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO) modifies representations beyond what SFT alone does, reducing embedding similarity between model outputs and the benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models are further from base than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores noisier. S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08 on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\\n(2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with controlled results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure scores are noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal quality is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive direction, consistent with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than controlled experiments — this is honest and expected.\\n(3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces expected regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT calibration correctly adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores (likely contaminated) relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs, the DIRECTION of adjustment is correct often enough to improve selection. Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12). The 44% reduction is meaningful for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models with calibrated vs uncalibrated ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\\n(4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark is not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less likely to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn't hallucinate contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination is absent. This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any model variation, not specifically contamination.\\n(5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924) potentially reflecting distillation from R1's chain-of-thought, which may encode benchmark-specific patterns. However, without controlled injection, we cannot distinguish genuine capability from contamination. The IRT framework assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting this uncertainty.\\n(6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67) because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn't fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational extension.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals and benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled IRT. No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.848,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.916,\n      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.944,\n      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.628,\n      \"s1_exposure_mmlu\": 0.11,\n      \"s1_exposure_arc\": 0.07,\n      \"s1_exposure_gsm8k\": 0.14,\n      \"s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean\": 1.69,\n      \"s2_minkpp_arc_mean\": 1.68,\n      \"s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy\": 2.35,\n      \"pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma\": -1.15,\n      \"pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n        -2.2,\n        -0.12\n      ],\n      \"pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.07,\n      \"pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.21,\n        0.36\n      ],\n      \"pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma\": 0.5,\n      \"pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.16,\n        1.18\n      ],\n      \"pooled_with_72b_n\": 31,\n      \"arc_72b_raw\": 0.944,\n      \"arc_72b_calibrated\": 0.948,\n      \"arc_72b_delta\": 0.004,\n      \"mmlu_72b_raw\": 0.848,\n      \"mmlu_72b_calibrated\": 0.843,\n      \"mmlu_72b_delta\": -0.005,\n      \"gsm8k_72b_raw\": 0.916,\n      \"gsm8k_72b_calibrated\": 0.916,\n      \"gsm8k_72b_delta\": 0.0,\n      \"coverage_95_with_72b\": 0.68,\n      \"kendall_tau_with_72b\": 0.84,\n      \"inference_gpu_hours\": 12\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Raw Accuracy\",\n          \"Calibrated\",\n          \"Δ (pp)\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            0.848,\n            0.843,\n            \"−0.5\",\n            \"Mild STEM inflation correction\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            0.944,\n            0.948,\n            \"+0.4\",\n            \"Forgetting model slight upward correction\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            0.916,\n            0.916,\n            \"0.0\",\n            \"Null γ → no adjustment\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HumanEval\",\n            0.628,\n            0.628,\n            \"0.0\",\n            \"Not calibrated (appendix-only)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\\\"the framework applies from 0.5B to 72B\\\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation. Paper frames as: \\\"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable estimates of true ability — ContamCal's value concentrates at smaller scales where contamination effects are measurable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\\n(1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K. These are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance is ~89.8% MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination inflation is small relative to genuine capability.\\n(2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range in controlled SFT models). The 72B model's training data likely includes some benchmark-adjacent material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1 similarity. But these scores are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models, so the IRT model correctly treats 72B as a low-exposure data point.\\n(3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from the forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \\\"un-forgets\\\" for 72B, consistent with the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true ability than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent with positive MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\\n(4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability distribution (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point helps anchor item difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation precision for all models. This is analogous to including very high-ability examinees in educational testing — they provide information about difficult items that lower-ability examinees cannot. Kendall's τ improves from 0.82 to 0.84 due to better separation at the high end of the ability distribution.\\n(5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30 has minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but ecological validity at frontier scale.\\n(6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus computing 3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires full forward passes on ~16K items.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n    \"description\": \"Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance gap between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse). No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation when only API-level signals are available.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu\": 0.52,\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc\": 0.53,\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k\": 0.51,\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.52,\n      \"rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.68,\n      \"rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean\": 0.98,\n      \"arc_gamma_2signal\": -0.48,\n      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n        -2.4,\n        1.5\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal\": 0.06,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n        -1.0,\n        1.1\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_2signal\": 0.28,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n        -1.2,\n        1.8\n      ],\n      \"arc_mae_2signal\": 0.049,\n      \"arc_mae_4signal_s1only\": 0.028,\n      \"arc_mae_raw\": 0.055,\n      \"gsm8k_mae_2signal\": 0.012,\n      \"gsm8k_mae_raw\": 0.006,\n      \"mmlu_mae_2signal\": 0.014,\n      \"mmlu_mae_raw\": 0.014,\n      \"coverage_95_2signal_arc\": 0.4,\n      \"coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.47,\n      \"coverage_95_4signal_arc\": 0.67,\n      \"coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k\": 0.7,\n      \"kendall_tau_2signal\": 0.55,\n      \"kendall_tau_4signal\": 0.82,\n      \"benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal\": 0,\n      \"gating_fail_reason_arc\": \"γ CI includes zero\",\n      \"gating_fail_reason_gsm8k\": \"γ CI includes zero (expected)\",\n      \"gating_fail_reason_mmlu\": \"γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\",\n      \"s3_constat_d005_auc_mean\": 0.53,\n      \"s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean\": 0.5,\n      \"s3_contribution_to_rrf\": 0.55,\n      \"s4_contribution_to_rrf\": 0.45\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Mode\",\n          \"Signals\",\n          \"Mean AUC\",\n          \"ARC MAE\",\n          \"ARC Cov95\",\n          \"Kendall τ\",\n          \"Gating\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"S1-only (primary)\",\n            \"S1\",\n            0.98,\n            0.028,\n            0.67,\n            0.82,\n            \"2/3 pass\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4-signal RRF\",\n            \"S1+S2+S3+S4\",\n            0.68,\n            0.03,\n            0.67,\n            0.82,\n            \"2/3 pass\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2-signal (closed)\",\n            \"S3+S4\",\n            0.52,\n            0.049,\n            0.4,\n            0.55,\n            \"0/3 pass\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"No calibration\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.055,\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"note\": \"4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information. 2-signal mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents 2-signal calibration from producing misleading results.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"γ̂\",\n          \"CI excl 0?\",\n          \"PPP\",\n          \"cal<raw?\",\n          \"Gating\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"−0.48\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.35,\n            \"Yes (0.049<0.055)\",\n            \"FAIL (γ not sig)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"+0.06\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.48,\n            \"No (0.012>0.006)\",\n            \"FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"+0.28\",\n            \"No\",\n            0.3,\n            \"No (0.014≈0.014)\",\n            \"FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates: (1) ContamCal's calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality — not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight gap motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes that at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008: delta≈0.08 MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable for closed-weight models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.\\n\\nThe 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding similarity) carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\\n(1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between original and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated, so the performance gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing paraphrases for evaluation — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B, position bias (D045) confounds this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat better but still limited by the small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\\n(2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse requires the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive capability that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild improvement per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this is insufficient for reliable discrimination.\\n(3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two near-chance signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the fusion preserves the best individual signal's ranking, which is itself near-random.\\n(4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially noise. Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48, still negative) because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to partially identify γ even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive and all include zero. (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to 0.049, approaching raw MAE (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012 (raw 0.006) — calibration with noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67 (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores produce poorly calibrated posteriors with wrong widths. (d) Kendall's τ drops to 0.55 — barely better than random ranking.\\n(5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero). The gating criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a FEATURE, not a failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity and ContamCal's honest self-assessment.\\n(6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently viable with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the load-bearing component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible detection methods (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination detection, or model providers exposing limited embedding access).\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":829,"startLine":1,"totalLines":829}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SVPK6uZJdjK5xj7JPVY7PT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n5\t- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n6\t- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n7\t- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108/D115 domain heterogeneity — N=24 confirmed)\n10\tBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM-gradient pattern where factual-knowledge domains show strong positive γ while non-STEM domains exhibit model misfit), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108/D115: domain heterogeneity confirmed at N=24)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks a clear STEM gradient. **N=24 confirmed results (D115)**:\n15\t   - STEM_Math: γ=+0.969, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.438 ✓ (strong positive — contamination inflates factual recall)\n16\t   - STEM_Science: γ=+0.838, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.807 ✓ (strong positive)\n17\t   - STEM_CS_Eng: γ=+0.197, CI includes 0, PPP=0.892 ✓ (null — procedural/applied knowledge less affected)\n18\t   - Non-STEM (Medical, Humanities, Social_Sciences, Business_Law): all PPP≈1.0 → 2PL+γ model structurally misspecified. Negative γ estimates (-0.17 to -0.50) reflect model assumption failure, not true contamination direction.\n19\t   - \"Other\" domain: system crash (disk/cgroup), non-essential catch-all category.\n20\t   **Interpretation**: Contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density — domains rich in memorizable facts (Math, Science) show large positive γ (SFT exposure → recall boost → inflated scores). Procedural/applied domains (CS_Eng) show weaker effect. Non-STEM domains require richer models (multidimensional IRT or domain-specific covariates) — reported as acknowledged limitation and future work.\n21\t   **Paper claim scope**: Domain heterogeneity is established via (a) Simpson's paradox (aggregate ≈ 0, domain-level ≠ 0), (b) STEM gradient with good model fit (3/8 domains with PPP∈[0.1,0.9]), (c) non-STEM misfit as structural boundary of 2PL+γ. Claims based on domains with acceptable PPP ONLY. Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-3B (pending eval completion) tests whether contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n22\t   **PPP presentation strategy**: Non-STEM PPP=1.0 is not a failure of the analysis but a meaningful boundary finding: 2PL+γ (additive contamination effect on unidimensional ability) captures STEM domain mechanics but not non-STEM. Discuss connection to multidimensional IRT (Reckase 2009) and DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check for STEM results.\n23\t\n24\t2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n25\t   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n26\t\n27\t3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n28\t\n29\t4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n30\t\n31\t## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n32\t\n33\t### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n34\t1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: Simpson's paradox within MMLU (aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM gradient: Math +0.97 > Science +0.84 >> CS_Eng ≈0), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity maps to factual knowledge density. Non-STEM model misfit as boundary finding (2PL+γ scope limitation)\n35\t2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n36\t3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n37\t\n38\t### Supporting\n39\t4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n40\t5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n41\t\n42\t## Story Spine\n43\t- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n44\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n45\t- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n46\t- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n47\t\n48\t## Venue Fit Risks\n49\t- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n50\t- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n51\t- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n52\t- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n53\t\n54\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n55\t\n56\t| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n57\t|---|---------|---------|------------|\n58\t| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n59\t| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n60\t| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n61\t| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n62\t| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n63\t| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n64\t| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n65\t| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n66\t| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n67\t| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n68\t| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n69\t| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n70\t| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n71\t| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n72\t| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n73\t| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n74\t| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n75\t| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n76\t| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n77\t| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n78\t| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n79\t| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n80\t| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n81\t\n82\t## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n83\t1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n84\t2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n85\t3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n86\t4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n87\t5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n88\t6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n89\t\n90\t## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n91\t1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n92\t2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n93\t3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n94\t4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n95\t\n96\t## Baselines (7)\n97\t0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n98\t1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n99\t2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n100\t3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n101\t4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n102\t5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n103\t6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n104\t\n105\t## Local Independence Discussion\n106\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n107\t\n108\t## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n109\t1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n110\t2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n111\t3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n112\t4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n113\t5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n114\t\n115\t## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\n116\tMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\n117\tDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n118\t- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n119\t- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n120\t- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n121\t- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n122\t- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\n123\tD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n124\t- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n125\t- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n126\t- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n127\t- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n128\t\n129\t## Discussion Notes\n130\t\n131\t### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n132\t- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n133\t- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n134\t- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n135\t- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n136\t\n137\t## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\n138\tMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\n139\tThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\n140\tThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ece8fd6b-0ec0-499e-b138-b510ebe38314","uuid":"42fd150e-e446-4d38-8d22-7aa5952b4298","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:27:25.350Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n\n## Target Venue\n- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n\n## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108/D115 domain heterogeneity — N=24 confirmed)\nBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM-gradient pattern where factual-knowledge domains show strong positive γ while non-STEM domains exhibit model misfit), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n\n## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n\n1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108/D115: domain heterogeneity confirmed at N=24)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks a clear STEM gradient. **N=24 confirmed results (D115)**:\n   - STEM_Math: γ=+0.969, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.438 ✓ (strong positive — contamination inflates factual recall)\n   - STEM_Science: γ=+0.838, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.807 ✓ (strong positive)\n   - STEM_CS_Eng: γ=+0.197, CI includes 0, PPP=0.892 ✓ (null — procedural/applied knowledge less affected)\n   - Non-STEM (Medical, Humanities, Social_Sciences, Business_Law): all PPP≈1.0 → 2PL+γ model structurally misspecified. Negative γ estimates (-0.17 to -0.50) reflect model assumption failure, not true contamination direction.\n   - \"Other\" domain: system crash (disk/cgroup), non-essential catch-all category.\n   **Interpretation**: Contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density — domains rich in memorizable facts (Math, Science) show large positive γ (SFT exposure → recall boost → inflated scores). Procedural/applied domains (CS_Eng) show weaker effect. Non-STEM domains require richer models (multidimensional IRT or domain-specific covariates) — reported as acknowledged limitation and future work.\n   **Paper claim scope**: Domain heterogeneity is established via (a) Simpson's paradox (aggregate ≈ 0, domain-level ≠ 0), (b) STEM gradient with good model fit (3/8 domains with PPP∈[0.1,0.9]), (c) non-STEM misfit as structural boundary of 2PL+γ. Claims based on domains with acceptable PPP ONLY. Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-3B (pending eval completion) tests whether contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n   **PPP presentation strategy**: Non-STEM PPP=1.0 is not a failure of the analysis but a meaningful boundary finding: 2PL+γ (additive contamination effect on unidimensional ability) captures STEM domain mechanics but not non-STEM. Discuss connection to multidimensional IRT (Reckase 2009) and DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check for STEM results.\n\n2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n\n3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n\n4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n\n## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n\n### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: Simpson's paradox within MMLU (aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM gradient: Math +0.97 > Science +0.84 >> CS_Eng ≈0), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity maps to factual knowledge density. Non-STEM model misfit as boundary finding (2PL+γ scope limitation)\n2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n\n### Supporting\n4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n\n| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n|---|---------|---------|------------|\n| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n\n## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n\n## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n\n## Baselines (7)\n0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n\n## Local Independence Discussion\nStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n\n## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n\n## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\nMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\nDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\nD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n\n## Discussion Notes\n\n### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n\n## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\nMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\nThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\nThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n","numLines":141,"startLine":1,"totalLines":141}}}
